<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="route-planner"
  xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1 http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1/gpx.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <name>Lakeland Way — Wild swims</name>
    <desc>87 wild swims near the route. Discovered from OpenStreetMap water features within 4km of the route, then curated. Positions are OSM centroids: accurate for tarns and pools, but flagged needsEntryPoint for large lakes where the centroid lies offshore.</desc>
  </metadata>
  <wpt lat="54.397863" lon="-3.286649">
    <ele>220.0</ele>
    <name>Blea Tarn</name>
    <desc>A small tarn on the Eskdale moor at 220m, quiet and rarely visited. Detour: +5.1km, +206m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.406232" lon="-3.250651">
    <ele>210.0</ele>
    <name>Eel Tarn</name>
    <desc>A boggy little tarn on the moor above Boot at 210m; peaty and mild. Detour: +4.2km, +137m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.411431" lon="-3.235193">
    <ele>306.0</ele>
    <name>Stony Tarn</name>
    <desc>Rocky-shored tarn at 306m on the Eskdale moor, harder to reach than nearby Eel Tarn. Detour: +8.1km, +317m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.428301" lon="-3.259506">
    <ele>254.0</ele>
    <name>Burnmoor Tarn</name>
    <desc>A wide shallow tarn on the moor between Eskdale and Wasdale at 254m. Peaty and comparatively mild; the surrounding ground is boggy, so use the firmer northern shore. Detour: +0.0km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.439266" lon="-3.292009">
    <ele>61.0</ele>
    <name>Wast Water</name>
    <desc>England&apos;s deepest lake at 79m, with no motorised craft and the screes plunging straight into it. Long shingle shore for easy entry; cold at any time of year. Detour: +7.8km, +231m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.455374" lon="-3.316741">
    <ele>404.0</ele>
    <name>Greendale Tarn</name>
    <desc>A quiet tarn at 404m between Middle Fell and Seatallan above Wasdale. Detour: +14.1km, +416m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.472317" lon="-3.294942">
    <ele>517.0</ele>
    <name>Low Tarn</name>
    <desc>A remote pair of small tarns at 517m on the flank of Yewbarrow above Wasdale. Detour: +6.0km, +489m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.472348" lon="-3.258305">
    <ele>116.0</ele>
    <name>Ritson Force</name>
    <desc>A short fall in woodland behind the Wasdale Head Inn, with a small pool beneath. Detour: +1.2km, +35m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.481574" lon="-3.298879">
    <ele>600.0</ele>
    <name>Scoat Tarn</name>
    <desc>A deep, cold tarn at 600m under Scoat Fell, one of the remotest on the route. Detour: +10.1km, +852m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.472261" lon="-3.219753">
    <ele>217.0</ele>
    <name>Emerald Pools</name>
    <desc>Green-tinted pools in the bed of Lingmell Beck below Wasdale Head, where the water has cut into clean rock. Cold and clear; easy entry from the shingle. Detour: +2.6km, +163m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.478084" lon="-3.202674">
    <ele>440.0</ele>
    <name>Styhead Tarn</name>
    <desc>Beneath Great Gable at 440m, right on the pass. Popular by day, quiet at either end of it. Mountain rescue stretcher box nearby. Detour: +1.4km, +49m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.488177" lon="-3.190660">
    <ele>302.0</ele>
    <name>Taylorgill Force</name>
    <desc>A 40m fall on Styhead Gill above Seathwaite, seen from the Styhead path. Detour: +4.3km, +204m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.471890" lon="-3.193005">
    <ele>602.0</ele>
    <name>Sprinkling Tarn</name>
    <desc>At 602m below Great End, one of the highest sizeable tarns in the district and a superb place to swim on a clear evening. Exposed — there is no shelter if the weather turns. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.442400" lon="-3.208612">
    <ele>474.0</ele>
    <name>Cam Spout</name>
    <desc>A long cascade at 474m where the upper Esk drops beneath Scafell. Detour: +7.5km, +520m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.458658" lon="-3.167308">
    <ele>557.0</ele>
    <name>Angle Tarn</name>
    <desc>Sitting at 557m under Bowfell and Rossett Pike, dark and deep with a boulder shore. Reached directly by the route over Rossett Gill. Detour: +0.0km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.444250" lon="-3.161208">
    <ele>724.0</ele>
    <name>Three Tarns</name>
    <desc>A cluster of small pools on the col at 724m between Bowfell and Crinkle Crags — more a place to dip than to swim, and utterly exposed. Detour: +4.4km, +470m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.438324" lon="-3.140617">
    <ele>310.0</ele>
    <name>Whorneyside Force</name>
    <desc>A clean fall on Buscoe Sike below Three Tarns, in upper Langdale. Detour: +9.0km, +943m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.451724" lon="-3.101073">
    <ele>360.0</ele>
    <name>Dungeon Ghyll Waterfall</name>
    <desc>Detour: +7.1km, +487m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.449604" lon="-3.096419">
    <ele>212.0</ele>
    <name>Dungeon Ghyll Force</name>
    <desc>A fall in a narrow cleft above Great Langdale, behind the old hotel. Detour: +8.8km, +841m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.459362" lon="-3.100687">
    <ele>475.0</ele>
    <name>Stickle Tarn</name>
    <desc>Dammed at 475m directly beneath the face of Pavey Ark, reached by a steep 40 minutes up Stickle Ghyll. The crag rising straight from the far shore is the draw. Detour: +6.6km, +491m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.480440" lon="-3.137482">
    <ele>209.0</ele>
    <name>Tray Dub</name>
    <desc>A quiet plunge pool on Langstrath Beck, a few metres off the path. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.481883" lon="-3.137750">
    <ele>203.0</ele>
    <name>Swan Dub</name>
    <desc>Small deep pool on Langstrath Beck, immediately beside the route. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.491915" lon="-3.132924">
    <ele>184.0</ele>
    <name>Black Moss Pot</name>
    <desc>A deep rock-walled pool in Langstrath, reached by a flat walk up the valley. Sheer sides give a natural jump of a few metres into water deep enough for it; a sloping slab at the downstream end is the easy way out. One of the best-known wild swims in the Lakes, and the route passes within 20m of it. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.508016" lon="-3.124697">
    <ele>124.0</ele>
    <name>Galleny Force</name>
    <desc>Known locally as the Fairy Glen — a chain of clear pools and small falls on Greenup Gill above Stonethwaite. Shallower and gentler than Black Moss Pot, with slabs to sit on. Directly on the route. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.519264" lon="-3.123420">
    <ele>411.0</ele>
    <name>Dock Tarn</name>
    <desc>A shallow tarn at 411m on the shelf above Stonethwaite, ringed by heather. Detour: +3.6km, +318m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.535317" lon="-3.121719">
    <ele>259.0</ele>
    <name>Watendlath Tarn</name>
    <desc>A tarn at 259m beside the hamlet of Watendlath, reached by a packhorse track. Detour: +4.8km, +297m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.526511" lon="-3.191458">
    <ele>504.0</ele>
    <name>Dalehead Tarn</name>
    <desc>A small tarn on the col at 504m below Dale Head, a common wild camp stop. Detour: +3.7km, +352m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.522538" lon="-3.165788">
    <ele>167.0</ele>
    <name>Scaleclose Force</name>
    <desc>A fall on the beck below Honister, close to the route. Detour: +1.0km, +45m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.504258" lon="-3.234004">
    <ele>487.0</ele>
    <name>Blackbeck Tarn</name>
    <desc>Just east of Innominate Tarn at 487m, a little larger and usually deserted. Detour: +2.9km, +208m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.505491" lon="-3.240770">
    <ele>528.0</ele>
    <name>Innominate Tarn</name>
    <desc>The tarn on Haystacks where Wainwright&apos;s ashes were scattered, at 528m. Small, peat-bedded and ringed by rock — the view down Ennerdale is the reason to stop. Detour: +3.4km, +261m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.529919" lon="-3.265554">
    <ele>104.0</ele>
    <name>Buttermere</name>
    <desc>No motorised craft, ringed by Haystacks, Fleetwith Pike and High Stile. Easy grassy entry from the shore path near the Gatesgarth end. One of the finest lake swims on the route. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.527351" lon="-3.290136">
    <ele>493.0</ele>
    <name>Bleaberry Tarn</name>
    <desc>In the corrie between Red Pike and High Stile at 493m, above Buttermere. Detour: +3.5km, +392m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.544983" lon="-3.249309">
    <ele>424.0</ele>
    <name>Moss Force</name>
    <desc>A stepped fall beside the Newlands Hause road, visible from the top of the pass. Detour: +5.3km, +286m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.541814" lon="-3.314638">
    <ele>243.0</ele>
    <name>Scale Force</name>
    <desc>The highest single drop in the Lake District at around 52m, in a mossy cleft above Crummock Water. The plunge pool is small and shaded. Detour: +2.1km, +109m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.558358" lon="-3.302403">
    <ele>99.0</ele>
    <name>Crummock Water</name>
    <desc>Larger and deeper than neighbouring Buttermere, with no powerboats. Scale Force, the highest fall in the Lakes, comes down into its western side. Detour: +6.8km, +28m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.580314" lon="-3.363899">
    <ele>204.0</ele>
    <name>Holme Force</name>
    <desc>A wooded fall above Loweswater, quiet and little visited. Detour: +8.8km, +198m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.583357" lon="-3.355940">
    <ele>121.0</ele>
    <name>Loweswater</name>
    <desc>The quietest of the western lakes at 121m, with no powerboats and a wooded shore. Detour: +8.6km, +77m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.578911" lon="-3.145973">
    <ele>75.0</ele>
    <name>Derwent Water</name>
    <desc>Broad and shallow at 75m with wooded islands; busy with launches near Keswick. Detour: +7.5km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.557850" lon="-3.138046">
    <ele>163.0</ele>
    <name>Lodore Falls</name>
    <desc>A fall in a wooded ravine at the head of Derwent Water; impressive only after rain. Detour: +6.1km, +68m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.567273" lon="-3.130235">
    <ele>170.0</ele>
    <name>Ashness Bridge</name>
    <desc>The much-photographed packhorse bridge on Watendlath Beck, with pools below. Detour: +3.5km, +87m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.555681" lon="-3.135056">
    <ele>219.0</ele>
    <name>Watendlath Beck</name>
    <desc>Falls on the beck between Watendlath and Derwent Water. Detour: +7.3km, +141m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.602286" lon="-3.078034">
    <ele>205.0</ele>
    <name>Tewet Tarn</name>
    <desc>A shallow field tarn at 205m near Castlerigg, with a view to Blencathra. Detour: +3.2km, +64m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.581691" lon="-2.929985">
    <ele>249.0</ele>
    <name>High Cascades</name>
    <desc>Upper falls in the Aira Beck glen. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.575128" lon="-2.887456">
    <ele>145.0</ele>
    <name>Ullswater</name>
    <desc>The second largest lake, with steamer traffic — swim only in sheltered bays such as Glencoyne, and wear a bright cap. Detour: +37.0km, +938m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.579303" lon="-2.929251">
    <ele>233.0</ele>
    <name>High Force</name>
    <desc>A fall in the Aira Beck glen above Ullswater, upstream of Aira Force. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.562967" lon="-2.873733">
    <ele>236.0</ele>
    <name>Lanty Tarn</name>
    <desc>Detour: +31.9km, +1605m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.576634" lon="-2.930656">
    <ele>201.0</ele>
    <name>Aira Force</name>
    <desc>A 20m fall in a landscaped glen above Ullswater, crossed by two stone bridges. Popular and well made — not a swim, but a good stop. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.561033" lon="-3.050705">
    <ele>235.0</ele>
    <name>Stybeck Waterfall</name>
    <desc>A fall on the fellside above Thirlmere near Stybeck Farm. Detour: +8.1km, +709m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.554766" lon="-3.051361">
    <ele>269.0</ele>
    <name>Fisherplace Gill Waterfall</name>
    <desc>A cascade on Fisherplace Gill above Thirlspot. Detour: +9.5km, +719m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.535140" lon="-3.017068">
    <ele>617.0</ele>
    <name>Brown Cove Tarn</name>
    <desc>A small dammed tarn at 617m in the cove north of Helvellyn. Detour: +5.5km, +292m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.528344" lon="-3.008854">
    <ele>717.0</ele>
    <name>Red Tarn</name>
    <desc>In Helvellyn&apos;s eastern corrie at 717m between Striding and Swirral Edges. Snow lingers here into spring; expect it to be bitter. Detour: +6.8km, +478m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.537865" lon="-2.953966">
    <ele>285.0</ele>
    <name>Lanty&apos;s Tarn</name>
    <desc>A small wooded tarn above Glenridding at 285m, dammed to supply ice to the estate. Sheltered and easy to reach — a good option in poor weather. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.507521" lon="-2.923946">
    <ele>161.0</ele>
    <name>Brothers Water</name>
    <desc>A small shallow water at 161m in Patterdale, with a reedy margin. Detour: +1.8km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.502068" lon="-2.879572">
    <ele>428.0</ele>
    <name>Hayeswater</name>
    <desc>A former reservoir at 428m under Gray Crag, now returned to a natural tarn. Detour: +0.3km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.482566" lon="-2.842269">
    <ele>455.0</ele>
    <name>Small Water</name>
    <desc>Below Nan Bield Pass at 455m, with stone shelters on its shore. Smaller and shallower than Blea Water just over the ridge. Detour: +5.5km, +368m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.488920" lon="-2.852351">
    <ele>486.0</ele>
    <name>Blea Water</name>
    <desc>The deepest tarn in England at around 63m, held in a textbook glacial corrie under High Street at 486m. Very cold, very clear, with a boulder shore and no easy shallows. Detour: +3.9km, +341m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.431981" lon="-2.833288">
    <ele>196.0</ele>
    <name>Force Jump</name>
    <desc>A pool below a fall on the River Kent above Staveley. Detour: +14.8km, +625m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.432689" lon="-2.951210">
    <ele>142.0</ele>
    <name>Stock Ghyll Force Waterfall</name>
    <desc>A 21m fall in woodland immediately above Ambleside. Detour: +1.7km, +61m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.461156" lon="-2.978492">
    <ele>242.0</ele>
    <name>Buckstones Jump</name>
    <desc>A pool on Rydal Beck, upstream in the valley behind Rydal. Detour: +3.1km, +149m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.452227" lon="-2.979696">
    <ele>146.0</ele>
    <name>High Fall</name>
    <desc>A fall in the Rydal Beck valley behind Rydal Hall. Detour: +0.7km, +35m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.447219" lon="-2.994644">
    <ele>57.0</ele>
    <name>Rydal Water</name>
    <desc>Small, shallow and sheltered at 57m, with easy shore access and a rock island to swim out to. Detour: +4.0km, +81m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.462203" lon="-3.005654">
    <ele>370.0</ele>
    <name>Alcock Tarn</name>
    <desc>A small dammed tarn at 370m above Grasmere, with a fine view down the vale. Detour: +3.6km, +254m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.469727" lon="-3.054192">
    <ele>179.0</ele>
    <name>Sourmilk Gill Falls</name>
    <desc>The cascade below Easedale Tarn, white against the fellside after rain. Detour: +5.8km, +107m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.469181" lon="-3.069350">
    <ele>283.0</ele>
    <name>Easedale Tarn</name>
    <desc>A 45-minute climb from Grasmere past Sour Milk Gill to 283m. Cold and clear, in a broad bowl under Tarn Crag. Detour: +8.8km, +221m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.449504" lon="-3.022421">
    <ele>64.0</ele>
    <name>Grasmere</name>
    <desc>Gentle shelving shore at 64m; busy in the day, calm early and late. Detour: +2.3km, +56m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.430354" lon="-3.011500">
    <ele>97.0</ele>
    <name>Loughrigg Tarn</name>
    <desc>A pretty lowland tarn at 97m below Loughrigg Fell, in pasture. Detour: +5.6km, +124m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.427755" lon="-3.029350">
    <ele>56.0</ele>
    <name>Elter Water</name>
    <desc>Shallow and reed-fringed at 56m, with the Langdale Pikes filling the view. Warmest water on the route by some margin. Detour: +2.1km, +13m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.421995" lon="-3.016517">
    <ele>70.0</ele>
    <name>Skelwith Force</name>
    <desc>A short but powerful fall on the Brathay near Elterwater, with a viewing platform. Detour: +5.2km, +20m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.419412" lon="-3.037337">
    <ele>92.0</ele>
    <name>Colwith Force</name>
    <desc>A 15m fall in woodland on the River Brathay between Little and Great Langdale. Detour: +4.3km, +140m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.419693" lon="-3.066548">
    <ele>104.0</ele>
    <name>Little Langdale Tarn</name>
    <desc>A quiet valley tarn at 104m with a reedy shore; much less visited than Elter Water. Detour: +2.3km, +64m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.390308" lon="-3.040939">
    <ele>171.0</ele>
    <name>Tom Ghyll Waterfalls</name>
    <desc>Falls on the outflow below Tarn Hows. Detour: +6.9km, +360m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.391680" lon="-3.033305">
    <ele>207.0</ele>
    <name>Tarn Hows</name>
    <desc>A landscaped tarn at 207m with a surfaced circular path. Very busy, and swimming is discouraged here. Detour: +6.6km, +172m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.397342" lon="-3.079845">
    <ele>295.0</ele>
    <name>Tilberthwaite Gill Waterfall</name>
    <desc>Falls in a narrow mined ravine above Tilberthwaite, on the route to Wetherlam. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.372717" lon="-3.087524">
    <ele>149.0</ele>
    <name>Coniston Waterfall</name>
    <desc>Falls on Church Beck in the Coppermines valley above Coniston. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.384129" lon="-3.111418">
    <ele>413.0</ele>
    <name>Levers Water</name>
    <desc>A dammed tarn at 413m in the Coppermines valley above Coniston, surrounded by mine workings. Deep, clear and cold. Detour: +0.8km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.374549" lon="-3.117781">
    <ele>546.0</ele>
    <name>Low Water</name>
    <desc>A shelf tarn at 546m directly below the summit of Coniston Old Man. Detour: +3.3km, +260m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.335032" lon="-3.074183">
    <ele>46.0</ele>
    <name>Coniston Water</name>
    <desc>Detour: +19.4km, +383m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.368980" lon="-3.131326">
    <ele>505.0</ele>
    <name>Goats Water</name>
    <desc>Squeezed between Dow Crag and Coniston Old Man at 505m, with a boulder shore beneath the crag. Sunless for much of the day. Detour: +3.8km, +199m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.360667" lon="-3.136620">
    <ele>565.0</ele>
    <name>Blind Tarn</name>
    <desc>A true corrie tarn at 565m on Dow Crag with no inlet or outlet stream — it is fed entirely by the fell around it. Detour: +0.9km, +54m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.379281" lon="-3.150259">
    <ele>372.0</ele>
    <name>Seathwaite Tarn</name>
    <desc>A dammed tarn at 372m in a remote hollow under Grey Friar in the Duddon fells. Detour: +7.3km, +356m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.422366" lon="-3.192164">
    <ele>194.0</ele>
    <name>Lingcove Beck Falls</name>
    <desc>Pools on Lingcove Beck just above its junction with the Esk, beside the old packhorse bridge. Detour: +7.7km, +281m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.422883" lon="-3.193924">
    <ele>210.0</ele>
    <name>Esk Falls</name>
    <desc>The upper Esk gorge below Lingcove Bridge, a sequence of deep emerald pools cut into bare rock with falls between them — the Tongue Pot area. Pebble entry, jumps possible, and arguably the finest river swimming in England. Worth timing the day around. Detour: +9.4km, +387m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.423000" lon="-3.191802">
    <ele>212.0</ele>
    <name>Lingcove Beck Falls (upper)</name>
    <desc>Detour: +7.9km, +302m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.388219" lon="-3.252579">
    <ele>227.0</ele>
    <name>Birker Force</name>
    <desc>A fall on the edge of Birker Fell above Eskdale. Detour: +4.9km, +203m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.390868" lon="-3.267857">
    <ele>55.0</ele>
    <name>Gill Force</name>
    <desc>Where the Esk narrows into a rock gorge below Doctor Bridge, with a deep green pool and slabs above it. Directly on the route in lower Eskdale. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.384022" lon="-3.272991">
    <ele>153.0</ele>
    <name>Stanley Force</name>
    <desc>A 20m fall in a wooded ravine off Eskdale, with a pool at its foot. The gorge path is slippery — care on the approach. On the route.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.361363" lon="-3.297380">
    <ele>234.0</ele>
    <name>Devoke Water</name>
    <desc>The largest tarn in the district at 234m, alone on Birker Fell with no fells immediately around it. Big skies, no shelter, rarely anyone there. Detour: +3.3km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
